  body: `
<p>Spoken English has its own grammar. It isn't wrong: it's adapted to real-time conversation. Understanding it is essential at C2, and using it makes you sound natural.</p>
<h2>Ellipsis: leaving words out</h2>
<p><s-en>Sounds good.</s-en> (It sounds good) · <s-en>Want a coffee?</s-en> (Do you want…) · <s-en>Not sure.</s-en> · <s-en>Been there, done that.</s-en></p>
<h2>Heads and tails</h2>
<p>Head (topic first): <s-en>That new café on the corner, have you tried it?</s-en> · Tail (comment at the end): <s-en>It's really good, that film.</s-en> · <s-en>He's clever, your brother.</s-en></p>
<h2>Vague language</h2>
<p><s-en>and stuff like that</s-en> · <s-en>or something</s-en> · <s-en>sort of / kind of</s-en> · <s-en>-ish</s-en> · <s-en>loads of</s-en> · <s-en>thingy, whatsit</s-en></p>
<h2>Reporting in conversation</h2>
<p><s-en>So she goes, “No way!” and I'm like, “Seriously?”</s-en> (informal: <b>go</b> and <b>be like</b> introduce speech)</p>
<h2>Response tokens</h2>
<p><s-en>Right.</s-en> · <s-en>Fair enough.</s-en> · <s-en>Tell me about it.</s-en> · <s-en>No way!</s-en> · <s-en>I know, right?</s-en> · <s-en>Absolutely.</s-en></p>
<div class="tip">Keep these for speech and informal messages. In formal writing, use complete sentences.</div>
`,
  body_fr: `
<p>L'anglais parlé a sa propre grammaire. Ce n'est pas une faute : elle est adaptée à la conversation en temps réel. La comprendre est indispensable au C2, et l'utiliser te rend naturelle.</p>
<h2>L'ellipse : on supprime des mots</h2>
<p><s-en>Sounds good.</s-en> (It sounds good : ça me va) · <s-en>Want a coffee?</s-en> (Do you want… : un café ?) · <s-en>Not sure.</s-en> · <s-en>Been there, done that.</s-en> (Déjà vu, déjà fait.)</p>
<h2>Thème en tête, commentaire en queue</h2>
<p>En tête (le sujet d'abord) : <s-en>That new café on the corner, have you tried it?</s-en> (Le nouveau café du coin, tu l'as essayé ?) · En queue (commentaire à la fin) : <s-en>It's really good, that film.</s-en> (Il est vraiment bien, ce film.) · <s-en>He's clever, your brother.</s-en></p>
<h2>Le langage vague</h2>
<p><s-en>and stuff like that</s-en> · <s-en>or something</s-en> · <s-en>sort of / kind of</s-en> · <s-en>-ish</s-en> · <s-en>loads of</s-en> · <s-en>thingy, whatsit</s-en> (machin, truc)</p>
<h2>Rapporter des paroles à l'oral</h2>
<p><s-en>So she goes, “No way!” and I'm like, “Seriously?”</s-en> (familier : <b>go</b> et <b>be like</b> introduisent des paroles, comme « elle fait… » / « et moi genre… »)</p>
<h2>Les petites réactions</h2>
<p><s-en>Right.</s-en> · <s-en>Fair enough.</s-en> (Ça se tient.) · <s-en>Tell me about it.</s-en> (À qui le dis-tu.) · <s-en>No way!</s-en> · <s-en>I know, right?</s-en> (Hein, c'est clair ?) · <s-en>Absolutely.</s-en></p>
<div class="tip">Garde-les pour l'oral et les messages informels. À l'écrit soutenu, fais des phrases complètes.</div>
`,
